1
00:01:56,483 --> 00:01:57,507
Oh, there he is.

2
00:02:01,421 --> 00:02:04,653
- This won't take long.
- I have a book.

3
00:02:13,166 --> 00:02:14,657
Peter.

4
00:02:15,469 --> 00:02:17,597
Oh, hi. Brian.

5
00:02:17,871 --> 00:02:19,231
What's it been?
About seven years?

6
00:02:19,373 --> 00:02:21,433
Hard to believe, isn't it?

7
00:02:21,608 --> 00:02:23,076
Sorry to hear about your father.

8
00:02:23,243 --> 00:02:24,575
- Thanks.
- Heart attack?

9
00:02:24,745 --> 00:02:25,940
Died in his sleep.

10
00:02:26,113 --> 00:02:27,877
Well, if he had to go...

11
00:02:28,048 --> 00:02:30,517
Yeah. It was best that way.

12
00:02:30,684 --> 00:02:32,364
Listen, I'm sorry we
had to meet like this,

13
00:02:32,519 --> 00:02:34,039
instead of over
breakfast or something.

14
00:02:34,187 --> 00:02:36,427
Gotta be in Philadelphia
day after tomorrow. The trial...

15
00:02:36,523 --> 00:02:38,651
It's okay, don't worry about it.

16
00:02:38,825 --> 00:02:41,351
I just need to tell
you a couple of things.

17
00:02:41,528 --> 00:02:45,795
I suppose I could have told
you in a letter or over the phone,

18
00:02:46,466 --> 00:02:48,560
but that wouldn't
have been right.

19
00:02:55,208 --> 00:02:56,904
I'm dying, Peter.

20
00:02:57,077 --> 00:03:00,275
My diabetes finally
caught up with me.

21
00:03:00,714 --> 00:03:02,307
Kidneys are failing.

22
00:03:02,482 --> 00:03:04,474
Eyesight is shot.

23
00:03:04,651 --> 00:03:07,712
I got 10 weeks, maybe 12.

24
00:03:08,255 --> 00:03:09,450
I'm sorry, Brian.

25
00:03:09,623 --> 00:03:13,151
I haven't told anyone,
not even Gloria.

26
00:03:13,326 --> 00:03:14,988
I mean, she knows I'm sick,

27
00:03:15,162 --> 00:03:17,188
but I lied about how long I had.

28
00:03:17,364 --> 00:03:20,732
I figured, why worry her when
there's nothing anybody can do.

29
00:03:21,468 --> 00:03:24,905
If you haven't even told your
own wife, why are you telling me?

30
00:03:25,806 --> 00:03:30,904
Because I'm about to petition the
court to reopen the Lloyd Prentice case.

31
00:03:31,078 --> 00:03:32,137
You can't do that.

32
00:03:32,312 --> 00:03:34,838
I don't want to die with that
case on my conscience, Peter.

33
00:03:35,015 --> 00:03:36,677
But I'll be disbarred.
I'll be ruined.

34
00:03:36,850 --> 00:03:39,445
An innocent man has
been in prison for nine years.

35
00:03:39,619 --> 00:03:42,350
Because of you. You
were my boss, remember?

36
00:03:42,522 --> 00:03:44,082
You forced me to
withhold that evidence.

37
00:03:44,224 --> 00:03:46,064
What happened to Lloyd
Prentice was your fault.

38
00:03:46,226 --> 00:03:47,285
I know.

39
00:03:47,461 --> 00:03:49,396
That's why I have to do this.

40
00:03:51,231 --> 00:03:52,893
- I'm sorry.
- Sorry?

41
00:03:53,066 --> 00:03:54,090
You're gonna be dead,

42
00:03:54,267 --> 00:03:57,260
and I'm gonna be paying for
what we did for the rest of my life?

43
00:03:57,537 --> 00:03:59,062
I'm very sorry.

44
00:04:00,607 --> 00:04:02,337
Brian, don't do this.

45
00:04:02,509 --> 00:04:04,603
Brian. Please.

46
00:04:04,778 --> 00:04:06,440
Brian!

47
00:04:22,028 --> 00:04:23,690
Two, please.

48
00:04:43,650 --> 00:04:46,051
Cheryl, Leanne's here.

49
00:04:46,219 --> 00:04:48,381
I'm gonna go over and say hi.

50
00:04:48,722 --> 00:04:50,486
Okay.

51
00:04:59,199 --> 00:05:00,462
Hello, Leanne.

52
00:05:02,369 --> 00:05:04,201
Hello, Peter.

53
00:05:04,638 --> 00:05:05,799
You look good.

54
00:05:07,774 --> 00:05:09,800
You look good too.

55
00:05:11,211 --> 00:05:13,771
- Is that Cheryl?
- Yeah.

56
00:05:13,947 --> 00:05:16,143
She looks good.

57
00:05:17,984 --> 00:05:21,011
- You gonna introduce us?
- You want me to?

58
00:05:21,188 --> 00:05:23,680
Um, ahem. Maybe
another time. Heh.

59
00:05:25,659 --> 00:05:27,855
I was sorry to hear
about your dad.

60
00:05:28,028 --> 00:05:31,055
- Did you get my note?
- Yeah. Thanks.

61
00:05:31,464 --> 00:05:33,504
I've been in town since
Monday settling his affairs.

62
00:05:33,633 --> 00:05:35,795
- Staying at his place?
- Yeah.

63
00:05:35,969 --> 00:05:38,666
Feels strange,
sleeping in my old bed.

64
00:05:40,140 --> 00:05:41,802
Still squeaks.

65
00:05:48,081 --> 00:05:50,107
It was nice to see you.

66
00:05:52,619 --> 00:05:53,643
You look good.

67
00:05:56,823 --> 00:05:58,314
Leanne.

68
00:06:01,628 --> 00:06:03,153
Take care.

69
00:06:04,564 --> 00:06:06,089
You too.

70
00:06:38,965 --> 00:06:41,400
Looks like Peter's got company.

71
00:06:51,678 --> 00:06:52,702
Peter.

72
00:06:52,879 --> 00:06:55,212
The Willeses are here.
We're gonna have some coffee.

73
00:07:00,253 --> 00:07:02,484
It's not what you
think. I swear.

74
00:07:20,907 --> 00:07:22,239
Ben.

75
00:07:23,376 --> 00:07:25,902
- I heard you were in jail.
- I just bailed myself out.

76
00:07:26,079 --> 00:07:28,207
- The answer's no.
- Oh, come on. Would you just wait?

77
00:07:28,381 --> 00:07:30,077
- I said no.
- But I didn't do it.

78
00:07:30,250 --> 00:07:32,515
- No.
- How...? I used to be family.

79
00:07:32,686 --> 00:07:34,655
How could you do this to me?

80
00:07:34,821 --> 00:07:37,518
Because I don't like you.

81
00:07:37,691 --> 00:07:38,750
I never have.

82
00:07:38,925 --> 00:07:42,362
I didn't like you before you ran off
with that girl and broke Leanne's heart.

83
00:07:42,529 --> 00:07:44,293
I'll tell you how
much I don't like you.

84
00:07:44,464 --> 00:07:47,491
I'd sooner eat a live
chicken than be your lawyer.

85
00:07:56,876 --> 00:07:59,641
So anyway, we'll get together,
we'll have sushi or a barbecue.

86
00:07:59,813 --> 00:08:00,933
All right. WOMAN: Okay.

87
00:08:01,014 --> 00:08:02,694
- Okay. I'll give you a call.
- Okay.

88
00:08:02,816 --> 00:08:04,341
- Bye-bye.
- Bye-bye.

89
00:08:04,517 --> 00:08:07,453
Hi. I'm Cheryl Atkinson.

90
00:08:07,620 --> 00:08:08,883
Hello, Cheryl.

91
00:08:09,055 --> 00:08:12,924
I knew you'd be coming in for a
massage. I checked at the desk.

92
00:08:13,093 --> 00:08:15,289
But can we please
sit down for a minute?

93
00:08:15,462 --> 00:08:17,727
Oh, sure. I'm right here.

94
00:08:24,504 --> 00:08:26,939
Peter would be furious with
me if he knew I was doing this.

95
00:08:27,474 --> 00:08:28,840
Doing what?

96
00:08:29,542 --> 00:08:31,135
He needs a good lawyer.

97
00:08:31,311 --> 00:08:33,712
I mean, a really good lawyer.

98
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:35,940
He asked your dad,
but he turned him down.

99
00:08:36,116 --> 00:08:37,675
So I heard.

100
00:08:37,851 --> 00:08:43,119
Leanne, Peter has never had anything
but good things to say about you.

101
00:08:43,656 --> 00:08:46,353
Well, he's always said
you're a good person.

102
00:08:46,526 --> 00:08:48,927
And the only attorney he's
ever met who has the ability

103
00:08:49,095 --> 00:08:53,430
to be as passionate about the law
as you are objective about the facts.

104
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:55,034
Well, that's nice to hear.

105
00:08:55,468 --> 00:08:56,629
He needs you.

106
00:08:58,471 --> 00:09:00,099
I'm really sorry,

107
00:09:00,273 --> 00:09:03,766
but I don't think an ex-wife is the
best lawyer an ex-husband can have.

108
00:09:03,943 --> 00:09:08,244
I know how awful it must
have been when he left you.

109
00:09:09,449 --> 00:09:11,475
And I know that if he
ever left me like that, I...

110
00:09:13,853 --> 00:09:15,685
I don't know what I'd do.

111
00:09:16,456 --> 00:09:18,096
You know, Cheryl, I
would really rather...

112
00:09:18,224 --> 00:09:21,058
What I'm trying to say is
that it was my fault, not his.

113
00:09:21,227 --> 00:09:25,130
If you're gonna blame
somebody, blame me.

114
00:09:25,932 --> 00:09:28,026
I knew he was married.

115
00:09:28,401 --> 00:09:30,427
I knew what we were
doing was wrong.

116
00:09:31,704 --> 00:09:33,639
But I'm the one
that wouldn't let go.

117
00:09:34,407 --> 00:09:36,638
Please don't punish
him for something I did.

118
00:09:45,885 --> 00:09:47,325
Thanks for doing this, Leanne.

119
00:09:47,387 --> 00:09:49,227
Wait a minute. I never
said I'd take your case.

120
00:09:49,389 --> 00:09:51,187
- We're just gonna discuss it.
- Okay.

121
00:09:51,357 --> 00:09:52,882
Okay.

122
00:09:53,560 --> 00:09:56,120
- Is your dad home?
- He went to dinner with a neighbor.

123
00:09:56,596 --> 00:09:58,326
He gonna be late?

124
00:09:59,866 --> 00:10:02,301
- We're divorced, Peter.
- I know.

125
00:10:02,469 --> 00:10:04,461
And I know it's my fault.

126
00:10:04,637 --> 00:10:06,071
But I think of you.

127
00:10:06,239 --> 00:10:07,707
And remember.

128
00:10:08,308 --> 00:10:10,436
I suppose we all do.

129
00:10:11,244 --> 00:10:14,214
Remember that time your father
almost caught us? Ha, ha, ha.

130
00:10:14,380 --> 00:10:16,540
When we were upstairs? That
was before we were married.

131
00:10:16,683 --> 00:10:19,118
- I tore my pants going out the window.
- You were so funny.

132
00:10:20,820 --> 00:10:22,789
We did have our
moments, didn't we?

133
00:10:22,956 --> 00:10:24,618
Yeah.

134
00:10:25,558 --> 00:10:27,652
And then they stopped.

135
00:10:30,930 --> 00:10:33,729
Okay. So...

136
00:10:37,770 --> 00:10:40,330
Why don't you tell me
what happened that night?

137
00:10:40,507 --> 00:10:42,874
Well, I had this dinner
meeting with dad's accountant.

138
00:10:45,044 --> 00:10:47,843
Got home around 7:30.

139
00:10:48,014 --> 00:10:49,983
I found Brian Morrell's
car in the driveway

140
00:10:50,150 --> 00:10:52,619
and Brian Morrell's
body in the living room

141
00:10:52,785 --> 00:10:55,653
with one of dad's kitchen
knives stuck in his back.

142
00:10:55,822 --> 00:10:58,262
A few minutes later, Cheryl and
this couple we know walked in,

143
00:10:58,391 --> 00:11:00,031
and right after that,
I called the police.

144
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:02,493
So Brian was already
dead when you got there.

145
00:11:02,662 --> 00:11:06,394
Someone must have
followed him in and killed him.

146
00:11:07,300 --> 00:11:09,769
Cheryl didn't have a key,
so I left the door unlocked.

147
00:11:09,936 --> 00:11:11,632
What was he doing there, anyway?

148
00:11:11,804 --> 00:11:13,739
I have no idea.

149
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:17,533
Well, you mean, out of the
blue, after what, seven years,

150
00:11:17,710 --> 00:11:21,147
he all of a sudden got the
urge to go and visit your dad?

151
00:11:21,447 --> 00:11:24,349
Uh, it hadn't been seven years.

152
00:11:25,785 --> 00:11:27,777
I saw him that morning.

153
00:11:28,188 --> 00:11:29,486
Oh, you did.

154
00:11:29,656 --> 00:11:32,096
He found out I was in town. He
called me and asked me to meet.

155
00:11:32,225 --> 00:11:34,456
Only time I could fit in was
right after my morning run.

156
00:11:34,961 --> 00:11:36,201
Why did he wanna meet with you?

157
00:11:36,329 --> 00:11:39,026
He told me he was dying.
Complications from his diabetes.

158
00:11:39,199 --> 00:11:41,668
He had about 10 weeks.

159
00:11:41,834 --> 00:11:42,961
What?

160
00:11:44,070 --> 00:11:45,470
Can we talk about
this over drinks?

161
00:11:45,605 --> 00:11:47,836
My treat. Come on. We'll
go to the Riviera Cafe.

162
00:11:48,007 --> 00:11:51,774
Remember the Riv? Have a
couple of nice, tall, cool margaritas.

163
00:11:52,745 --> 00:11:54,077
You know what?

164
00:11:54,247 --> 00:11:55,306
Don't do this.

165
00:11:55,481 --> 00:11:56,505
What?

166
00:11:56,683 --> 00:11:59,881
What's the problem with
us having a couple of drinks?

167
00:12:00,787 --> 00:12:02,312
Cheryl.

168
00:12:02,555 --> 00:12:05,218
She loves you very much.

169
00:12:05,391 --> 00:12:07,121
So don't do this, okay?

170
00:12:11,364 --> 00:12:14,596
So you'll take my case? Please?

171
00:12:20,640 --> 00:12:22,336
My fee is $80,000.

172
00:12:23,409 --> 00:12:25,810
Plus funeral expenses.

173
00:12:26,145 --> 00:12:27,272
Funeral expenses?

174
00:12:28,548 --> 00:12:30,642
My dad's gonna kill me.

175
00:12:47,100 --> 00:12:49,069
I can't believe it.

176
00:12:49,235 --> 00:12:51,033
I can't believe it.

177
00:12:51,204 --> 00:12:52,604
Now, you're bright.

178
00:12:52,772 --> 00:12:55,207
Very bright. I know
we've had our differences,

179
00:12:55,375 --> 00:12:59,244
but I've always thought you were
the brightest woman I ever met.

180
00:12:59,412 --> 00:13:01,005
Dad, we didn't meet.
I'm your daughter.

181
00:13:01,180 --> 00:13:04,309
- Well...
- And I don't think he did it.

182
00:13:04,484 --> 00:13:07,921
I mean, God knows, he has a
few less than desirable qualities.

183
00:13:08,087 --> 00:13:09,350
- Yeah.
- But he's no murderer.

184
00:13:09,522 --> 00:13:10,785
He's a jackass.

185
00:13:10,957 --> 00:13:13,153
And a jackass is
capable of anything.

186
00:13:13,326 --> 00:13:15,727
Oh, Dad, come on.

187
00:13:15,895 --> 00:13:16,919
All right.

188
00:13:17,096 --> 00:13:19,258
If Peter had killed
Brian Morrell,

189
00:13:19,432 --> 00:13:23,927
he would have invented the
most elaborate alibi in history.

190
00:13:24,103 --> 00:13:25,969
You know how devious he can be.

191
00:13:26,139 --> 00:13:27,163
Yeah.

192
00:13:27,907 --> 00:13:29,773
You know what I mean.

193
00:13:29,942 --> 00:13:31,877
Dad, I know what I'm doing.

194
00:13:32,045 --> 00:13:33,570
You wanna get
him back, don't you?

195
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:36,413
- No, I'm not.
- Then, why are you doing it?

196
00:13:36,582 --> 00:13:39,177
Because I think he's innocent,
and I think I can prove it.

197
00:13:39,352 --> 00:13:41,378
He's a liar and a cheat.

198
00:13:42,221 --> 00:13:46,022
He hurt you, and squandered
10 years of your life.

199
00:13:46,359 --> 00:13:48,919
Well, we had a
couple of good years.

200
00:13:49,095 --> 00:13:51,326
It's wrong for you to
go into the courtroom

201
00:13:51,497 --> 00:13:52,931
with this kind of baggage.

202
00:13:53,099 --> 00:13:55,125
It's not healthy for you.

203
00:13:55,301 --> 00:13:56,894
Not even for him.

204
00:13:57,070 --> 00:13:59,699
Something's got to
give, and you know it.

205
00:13:59,872 --> 00:14:01,602
Dad, I know this is awkward,

206
00:14:01,774 --> 00:14:04,972
you turning down the
case, and my taking it.

207
00:14:05,144 --> 00:14:08,603
- But I can handle it.
- He's a bum.

208
00:14:08,781 --> 00:14:10,750
He's a bum.

209
00:14:10,917 --> 00:14:12,613
He's a bum.

210
00:14:12,785 --> 00:14:14,879
He doesn't deserve you.

211
00:14:15,521 --> 00:14:19,617
Will you just try to be civil
for the duration? Please.

212
00:14:27,266 --> 00:14:29,026
Where are you going?
You gotta eat breakfast.

213
00:14:29,168 --> 00:14:30,693
I'm not hungry.

214
00:14:32,071 --> 00:14:34,267
He's a bum!

215
00:14:44,217 --> 00:14:45,981
- Please, sit down.
- Thank you.

216
00:14:46,152 --> 00:14:49,316
It was so gracious of you
to let me drop by like this.

217
00:14:49,489 --> 00:14:52,049
Frankly, it's a nice
change of pace.

218
00:14:52,225 --> 00:14:54,626
There's just so much
that needs to be done.

219
00:14:54,794 --> 00:14:58,526
Funerals are so complicated.
So many details to attend to.

220
00:14:58,698 --> 00:15:01,395
Seems like I've been
on the phone all morning.

221
00:15:02,602 --> 00:15:05,663
Brian was used to
dealing with minutiae.

222
00:15:05,838 --> 00:15:06,862
I'm not.

223
00:15:09,242 --> 00:15:12,144
No. I'll let the
service answer it.

224
00:15:12,545 --> 00:15:13,740
Let's talk.

225
00:15:15,715 --> 00:15:17,809
We've met before, haven't we?

226
00:15:17,984 --> 00:15:19,111
Yes, we have.

227
00:15:19,285 --> 00:15:22,847
It was about 10 years
ago, at your Christmas party.

228
00:15:23,022 --> 00:15:24,462
Peter and I had
just started dating.

229
00:15:24,624 --> 00:15:27,753
Oh, yes. I remember.
You two were so happy.

230
00:15:27,927 --> 00:15:30,158
It's hard to believe
that it didn't work out.

231
00:15:30,897 --> 00:15:32,729
Well, those things happen.

232
00:15:32,899 --> 00:15:35,425
I don't know what
gets into some men.

233
00:15:35,601 --> 00:15:38,435
Brian was faithful
to me for 33 years.

234
00:15:39,672 --> 00:15:42,232
Mrs. Morrell, did your
husband have any enemies?

235
00:15:42,408 --> 00:15:45,674
Or was there anyone with whom he
wasn't getting along particularly well?

236
00:15:45,845 --> 00:15:47,541
Not that I'm aware of.

237
00:15:47,713 --> 00:15:49,238
Although, he was
a criminal attorney.

238
00:15:49,415 --> 00:15:51,782
I'm sure he was always
ruffling somebody's feathers.

239
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:58,419
Was he slowing down or easing out
of his practice, in view of his illness?

240
00:15:59,192 --> 00:16:02,219
No, Brian refused to let his
diabetes interfere with his work.

241
00:16:02,395 --> 00:16:05,456
He wasn't planning on retiring
for at least another two years.

242
00:16:06,399 --> 00:16:08,834
He told Peter he only
had 10 weeks to live.

243
00:16:10,269 --> 00:16:11,293
He what?

244
00:16:12,171 --> 00:16:14,504
Mrs. Morrell, your
husband was terminally ill.

245
00:16:14,674 --> 00:16:16,336
The autopsy confirmed that.

246
00:16:18,911 --> 00:16:21,779
Well, he'd been
having some problems,

247
00:16:21,948 --> 00:16:24,008
but he told me it
was nothing serious,

248
00:16:24,183 --> 00:16:28,848
that he had anywhere from
another two to five good years left.

249
00:16:29,222 --> 00:16:30,742
I mean, he hadn't
seen Peter for years.

250
00:16:30,890 --> 00:16:32,770
Why would he tell Peter
he was dying and not me?

251
00:16:33,559 --> 00:16:35,687
I'm just wondering
that same thing.

252
00:16:36,762 --> 00:16:39,254
He was so quiet when he
came back to the car, but I assu...

253
00:16:39,432 --> 00:16:40,991
Came back to the car?

254
00:16:41,167 --> 00:16:43,295
I went with him when the
two of them met in the park,

255
00:16:43,469 --> 00:16:46,166
and he barely said two
words the whole way home.

256
00:16:46,339 --> 00:16:48,570
But I assumed it was because
of the argument they had.

257
00:16:50,042 --> 00:16:51,135
They had an argument?

258
00:16:51,310 --> 00:16:52,676
Yes. I saw it from the car.

259
00:16:52,845 --> 00:16:54,211
What were they arguing about?

260
00:16:54,380 --> 00:16:56,178
I couldn't make out
what they were saying.

261
00:16:56,349 --> 00:17:00,116
But when I asked Brian about it on
the way home, he wouldn't answer me.

262
00:17:00,286 --> 00:17:01,948
But Peter was really upset.

263
00:17:02,121 --> 00:17:04,522
At one point, he even grabbed
Brian by the arm and shook him.

264
00:17:05,024 --> 00:17:06,117
- He did?
- Yes, he did.

265
00:17:06,292 --> 00:17:09,490
He was angry. He was very angry.

266
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:15,866
I got it.

267
00:17:19,605 --> 00:17:20,766
We need to talk.

268
00:17:20,940 --> 00:17:23,432
I'm sorry. This conversation
should be between us.

269
00:17:23,609 --> 00:17:25,049
I don't have any
secrets from Cheryl.

270
00:17:25,211 --> 00:17:29,546
- Apparently, you have some for me.
- I have some errands to run.

271
00:17:42,895 --> 00:17:44,193
You lied to me.

272
00:17:44,363 --> 00:17:45,443
What are you talking about?

273
00:17:45,598 --> 00:17:47,157
You had an argument with Brian.

274
00:17:47,333 --> 00:17:48,892
- Who told you that?
- Gloria.

275
00:17:49,068 --> 00:17:50,988
- Well, she couldn't...
- She was in Brian's car.

276
00:17:51,103 --> 00:17:52,264
She saw you two arguing.

277
00:17:53,439 --> 00:17:56,341
We weren't arguing. We were
having an animated discussion.

278
00:17:56,509 --> 00:17:58,102
About what?

279
00:17:59,478 --> 00:18:00,810
I don't remember.

280
00:18:01,414 --> 00:18:03,383
Well, you damn
well better remember,

281
00:18:03,549 --> 00:18:05,950
because Gloria Morrell
just torpedoed our best

282
00:18:06,118 --> 00:18:09,179
and maybe our only defense
that you had no motive.

283
00:18:09,355 --> 00:18:11,119
I had no motive.

284
00:18:11,290 --> 00:18:14,260
What she saw is going to
suggest to the jury that you did.

285
00:18:14,427 --> 00:18:17,488
So why don't you
just level with me.

286
00:18:19,131 --> 00:18:23,967
Well, it's just that it puts Brian
in such a bad light. That's all.

287
00:18:24,403 --> 00:18:25,701
What does?

288
00:18:26,238 --> 00:18:28,707
Well, he told me he was dying,

289
00:18:30,042 --> 00:18:33,843
and then he asked me to
do him this one final favor.

290
00:18:34,013 --> 00:18:36,693
He told me this good friend of
his in Philadelphia, this accountant,

291
00:18:36,849 --> 00:18:39,216
was being brought up
on embezzlement charges.

292
00:18:39,385 --> 00:18:44,346
He wanted me to do whatever it
took to get the DA to drop the case.

293
00:18:44,523 --> 00:18:45,889
And I mean whatever it took.

294
00:18:46,058 --> 00:18:49,426
- He wanted you to bribe someone?
- Can you believe that?

295
00:18:54,200 --> 00:18:55,998
Go ahead.

296
00:18:57,036 --> 00:19:00,564
Well, I mean, when he asked
me to do that, I just lost it, Leanne.

297
00:19:00,740 --> 00:19:02,208
I blew.

298
00:19:02,375 --> 00:19:07,609
I couldn't believe that he'd think that
I'd even consider bribing someone.

299
00:19:07,780 --> 00:19:14,482
And this was a man who I
used to respect and revere.

300
00:19:22,395 --> 00:19:25,024
That's what you
were arguing about?

301
00:19:25,831 --> 00:19:27,026
I refused.

302
00:19:27,199 --> 00:19:32,069
We argued, and
he ran off in a huff.

303
00:19:34,006 --> 00:19:35,770
Maybe he wasn't
thinking straight

304
00:19:35,941 --> 00:19:38,137
because he was on some
medication or something.

305
00:19:38,310 --> 00:19:39,869
I don't know.

306
00:19:40,179 --> 00:19:45,174
I never said anything about it
before because the guy's dead.

307
00:19:50,389 --> 00:19:53,985
So this accountant in
Philadelphia, what's his name?

308
00:19:54,160 --> 00:19:56,129
He never told me.

309
00:19:57,663 --> 00:19:59,791
Well, then, Peter, we
have no way of proving

310
00:19:59,965 --> 00:20:02,127
that that was the
reason you were arguing.

311
00:20:02,301 --> 00:20:06,500
Hon, I swear to you, I had
no reason to kill Brian Morrell.

312
00:20:07,206 --> 00:20:08,686
Maybe you should
tell that to the DA,

313
00:20:08,841 --> 00:20:12,744
because he is gonna do everything
in his power to come up with a reason.

314
00:20:12,912 --> 00:20:14,642
Let him.

315
00:20:46,345 --> 00:20:47,369
Hello, Peter.

316
00:20:47,546 --> 00:20:49,071
Hello, Mr. Ross.

317
00:20:49,248 --> 00:20:52,150
No offense, but I was rather
hoping I'd never see you again.

318
00:20:52,318 --> 00:20:54,082
The feeling was mutual.

319
00:20:54,253 --> 00:20:56,093
Brian was planning to
reopen the Prentice case.

320
00:20:56,255 --> 00:20:58,053
- Did you know that?
- Yes.

321
00:20:58,224 --> 00:20:59,556
So you did kill him.

322
00:20:59,725 --> 00:21:01,091
In my own home?

323
00:21:01,260 --> 00:21:02,922
What kind of an idiot
do you think I am?

324
00:21:03,095 --> 00:21:04,893
I heard you were interrupted.

325
00:21:05,064 --> 00:21:06,965
I didn't kill him.

326
00:21:07,133 --> 00:21:08,931
Now, what do you want?

327
00:21:09,101 --> 00:21:11,070
The bottom line here is
neither of us can afford

328
00:21:11,237 --> 00:21:13,297
to have the Prentice
case dredged up.

329
00:21:13,472 --> 00:21:15,270
No one's gonna dredge it up.

330
00:21:15,441 --> 00:21:18,309
What did you do with the
evidence you withheld from the DA?

331
00:21:18,477 --> 00:21:19,638
Brian destroyed it.

332
00:21:19,812 --> 00:21:21,572
What if he didn't,
Peter? What if he kept it?

333
00:21:21,714 --> 00:21:26,049
And what if, in his zeal to find a motive
for Brian's murder, this DA finds it?

334
00:21:26,218 --> 00:21:27,277
It's not gonna happen.

335
00:21:27,453 --> 00:21:30,252
If it does, we'll
both go to prison.

336
00:21:30,422 --> 00:21:32,288
Only, I'll get out
after five years.

337
00:21:32,458 --> 00:21:34,893
You will be lucky
to get out after 20.

338
00:21:36,428 --> 00:21:37,589
What's your point?

339
00:21:37,763 --> 00:21:41,632
My point is if your case goes
to trial, we'll both be destroyed.

340
00:21:41,801 --> 00:21:45,260
But if you disappear before
there can even be a trial,

341
00:21:45,437 --> 00:21:47,804
chances are we'll both
live happily ever after.

342
00:21:49,575 --> 00:21:52,409
- I don't have that kind of money.
- I do.

343
00:21:53,846 --> 00:21:56,782
This will open locker
number 422 at the airport.

344
00:21:56,949 --> 00:21:58,389
In it, you'll find
a million dollars

345
00:21:58,551 --> 00:22:01,316
and a one-way ticket
to the Cayman Islands.

346
00:22:01,487 --> 00:22:03,012
Go with God.

347
00:22:03,189 --> 00:22:04,350
Today.

348
00:22:37,690 --> 00:22:39,610
- Where are you going?
- What are you doing here?

349
00:22:39,758 --> 00:22:41,838
I forgot the dress I was
gonna... What are you doing?

350
00:22:41,927 --> 00:22:44,158
- I gotta go.
- Go?

351
00:22:44,330 --> 00:22:46,060
- Go where?
- I can't tell you.

352
00:22:46,232 --> 00:22:47,312
Well, what about the trial?

353
00:22:47,466 --> 00:22:49,731
- The trial's over.
- The trial's over?

354
00:22:49,902 --> 00:22:51,928
Well, you're running.
They'll think you did it.

355
00:22:52,104 --> 00:22:53,304
What, was I gonna find a note?

356
00:22:53,472 --> 00:22:56,632
It's better you don't know where I am.
You're the first one the police will ask.

357
00:22:56,775 --> 00:22:59,711
Peter, you're innocent.
Why are you doing this?

358
00:23:00,913 --> 00:23:02,609
I'll call.

359
00:23:03,482 --> 00:23:04,882
Peter.

360
00:23:05,050 --> 00:23:09,681
Peter, please don't
do this. It's wrong.

361
00:23:22,601 --> 00:23:26,504
Yeah, Matlock house.

362
00:23:27,740 --> 00:23:31,734
What? No, no,
no. She's not here.

363
00:23:31,911 --> 00:23:35,177
Yeah. Yeah, I know who you are.

364
00:23:37,349 --> 00:23:38,783
What?

365
00:23:40,019 --> 00:23:41,419
When?

366
00:23:44,023 --> 00:23:46,117
You betcha I'll tell her.

367
00:23:57,336 --> 00:24:01,034
<i>Agent Merritt, please
contact extension 2100.</i>

368
00:24:01,206 --> 00:24:05,576
<i>Agent Merritt, please
contact extension 2100.</i>

369
00:24:34,106 --> 00:24:35,130
<i>Ladies and gentlemen,</i>

370
00:24:35,307 --> 00:24:38,539
<i>we are now boarding
all seats on Flight 310.</i>

371
00:24:38,711 --> 00:24:42,739
<i>Boarding all seats
on Flight 310.</i>

372
00:25:16,915 --> 00:25:18,315
Come on.

373
00:25:24,923 --> 00:25:26,653
Leanne?

374
00:25:27,259 --> 00:25:30,457
- Leanne, you here?
- Dad, where'd you go?

375
00:25:31,630 --> 00:25:33,098
Tell her.

376
00:25:36,835 --> 00:25:40,863
- What's going on?
- Leanne, I screwed up.

377
00:25:41,040 --> 00:25:42,565
What?

378
00:25:47,079 --> 00:25:48,513
What?

379
00:25:49,114 --> 00:25:50,707
Ten years ago,

380
00:25:50,883 --> 00:25:54,445
when I was starting out as
an associate with Brian's firm...

381
00:25:55,721 --> 00:25:58,885
- Do you remember the Prentice case?
- Yes, of course, Peter.

382
00:25:59,058 --> 00:26:02,620
Lloyd Prentice was convicted of
robbing a bank and killing a guard.

383
00:26:02,795 --> 00:26:05,287
Automatic life sentence.
That's how we met.

384
00:26:05,464 --> 00:26:07,504
I was the prosecutor, you
were the defense attorney.

385
00:26:07,900 --> 00:26:09,493
I won, you lost.

386
00:26:10,135 --> 00:26:13,071
I lost that case
intentionally, Leanne.

387
00:26:13,772 --> 00:26:15,866
It was Brian's idea.

388
00:26:16,041 --> 00:26:17,441
I mean, he made
me suppress evidence

389
00:26:17,576 --> 00:26:19,545
that would have
given Prentice an alibi.

390
00:26:19,711 --> 00:26:22,977
I mean, we handed the poor
guy to you on a silver platter.

391
00:26:25,017 --> 00:26:26,986
You suppressed evidence?

392
00:26:28,821 --> 00:26:31,791
That's what Brian and I were
arguing about that morning.

393
00:26:31,957 --> 00:26:33,653
He wanted to die with
a clear conscience,

394
00:26:33,826 --> 00:26:35,385
so he was gonna get
the case reopened.

395
00:26:35,561 --> 00:26:37,962
I was trying to
talk him out of it.

396
00:26:39,998 --> 00:26:42,968
You know me. When I
screw up, I screw up really big.

397
00:26:46,872 --> 00:26:50,309
Why did Brian wanna lose the
case? What was in it for him?

398
00:26:52,878 --> 00:26:56,872
The president of the bank
that got robbed, William Ross,

399
00:26:57,049 --> 00:27:00,110
he was an old buddy of Brian's.

400
00:27:00,752 --> 00:27:04,780
He was involved in the robbery. In
fact, Ross planned the whole thing.

401
00:27:04,957 --> 00:27:07,119
Not the killing of the
guard. That wasn't planned.

402
00:27:07,292 --> 00:27:08,726
Suddenly, he had to make sure

403
00:27:08,894 --> 00:27:10,658
that somebody got
arrested and convicted

404
00:27:10,829 --> 00:27:13,424
so that the guy who he hired
for the robbery, the real murderer,

405
00:27:13,599 --> 00:27:14,999
wouldn't be tempted to talk.

406
00:27:15,167 --> 00:27:16,760
So he framed Lloyd Prentice

407
00:27:16,935 --> 00:27:20,804
and then got Brian to get you
to deliberately lose the case.

408
00:27:21,740 --> 00:27:23,265
Right.

409
00:27:26,612 --> 00:27:28,376
Well, how much did they pay you?

410
00:27:28,547 --> 00:27:32,040
A hundred thousand
for me, 200,000 for Brian.

411
00:27:33,619 --> 00:27:37,386
So then, the house we were
able to buy after we got married

412
00:27:37,556 --> 00:27:39,457
and the time-share in Cape Cod?

413
00:27:39,625 --> 00:27:42,185
The money came
from William Ross.

414
00:27:42,361 --> 00:27:44,728
Because of you, an
innocent man went to prison

415
00:27:44,897 --> 00:27:47,890
and a murderer went scot-free.

416
00:27:48,066 --> 00:27:50,900
Oh, well, now I understand why
you wanted me to be your attorney.

417
00:27:51,069 --> 00:27:53,265
You figured if I found
out, I wouldn't tell.

418
00:27:53,438 --> 00:27:55,398
- Leanne...
- You know, I have had enough of this.

419
00:27:55,541 --> 00:27:57,567
- I want you out of this house.
- Leanne, please...

420
00:27:57,743 --> 00:27:59,575
No. Listen. Did you
hear what I said?

421
00:28:00,179 --> 00:28:02,671
I want you out.

422
00:28:26,805 --> 00:28:28,774
Gonna shove over?

423
00:28:37,783 --> 00:28:40,514
Don't tell me you've been
pulling weeds all this time.

424
00:28:40,686 --> 00:28:42,052
Oh, yeah.

425
00:28:42,221 --> 00:28:46,454
Got our weeds, the neighbors'
weeds, weeds from the whole block.

426
00:28:48,860 --> 00:28:50,556
How you doing?

427
00:28:51,296 --> 00:28:53,128
I don't know.

428
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:56,320
When the Prentice
trial was over,

429
00:28:56,468 --> 00:29:00,428
Peter took me to dinner to
prove he wasn't a sore loser.

430
00:29:00,606 --> 00:29:03,132
Four months later,
we got married.

431
00:29:04,142 --> 00:29:06,976
Our whole life together
was founded on a lie.

432
00:29:07,145 --> 00:29:12,516
I don't know how I married him. I
mean, why didn't I see what he was?

433
00:29:13,852 --> 00:29:18,347
Well, you were young,
and he was good-looking.

434
00:29:18,523 --> 00:29:22,051
You both had the
practice of law in common.

435
00:29:23,061 --> 00:29:25,223
We were great in bed.

436
00:29:25,864 --> 00:29:27,526
Whatever.

437
00:29:28,200 --> 00:29:30,465
That's all there really was.

438
00:29:30,636 --> 00:29:37,133
He was so impulsive
and sneaky. Selfish.

439
00:29:38,810 --> 00:29:42,212
But to me, that made
him boyishly charming.

440
00:29:42,381 --> 00:29:44,612
Where was my brain?

441
00:29:45,617 --> 00:29:49,247
Oh, you probably got an inkling

442
00:29:49,421 --> 00:29:51,686
of what he was really
like every now and then.

443
00:29:51,857 --> 00:29:56,557
But a lot of times when a
person's with another person

444
00:29:56,728 --> 00:29:58,754
for a long time,

445
00:29:58,930 --> 00:30:04,164
it's hard to recognize or even
see what you're looking at.

446
00:30:05,337 --> 00:30:09,138
- You really think so?
- Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah, yeah.

447
00:30:09,308 --> 00:30:14,576
Whenever he acted like the
certifiable jackass he really is,

448
00:30:14,746 --> 00:30:18,012
why, you cared for him,
and you couldn't recognize it.

449
00:30:18,917 --> 00:30:21,386
How could I not see it? I...

450
00:30:21,553 --> 00:30:24,751
Well, it happens to people.

451
00:30:24,923 --> 00:30:27,222
Don't be hard on yourself.

452
00:30:29,027 --> 00:30:32,327
I can't defend him, Dad. At this
point, I can't even look at him.

453
00:30:32,497 --> 00:30:36,366
Oh, that's all right.
There are other lawyers.

454
00:30:40,872 --> 00:30:43,000
I'll get that.

455
00:30:53,685 --> 00:30:55,620
Yeah, Ben Matlock.

456
00:30:56,855 --> 00:30:58,983
Yeah, Al, what's up?

457
00:31:02,127 --> 00:31:05,120
- No kidding.
- I'm gonna go on home.

458
00:31:06,965 --> 00:31:09,400
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

459
00:31:09,568 --> 00:31:12,128
Thanks for the call,
Al. I appreciate it.

460
00:31:12,304 --> 00:31:15,172
- Well, I'll be.
- What?

461
00:31:15,974 --> 00:31:19,240
That was the desk sergeant
at downtown precinct.

462
00:31:19,411 --> 00:31:21,175
Peter's there.

463
00:31:21,346 --> 00:31:22,871
He just confessed.

464
00:31:23,048 --> 00:31:25,415
- To the murder?
- No.

465
00:31:25,584 --> 00:31:29,282
To obstruction of justice
in the Lloyd Prentice case.

466
00:31:30,255 --> 00:31:32,850
Huh, that took guts.

467
00:31:33,525 --> 00:31:35,790
I didn't think he had any.

468
00:31:55,680 --> 00:31:57,478
You heard, huh?

469
00:31:58,483 --> 00:32:01,976
- Did you talk to the DA yet?
- For about an hour.

470
00:32:02,854 --> 00:32:03,878
Well, after ten years,

471
00:32:04,055 --> 00:32:06,752
you finally managed
to impress my dad.

472
00:32:07,959 --> 00:32:09,291
What about you?

473
00:32:11,963 --> 00:32:14,194
I really wanna hate you.

474
00:32:23,675 --> 00:32:25,041
But...

475
00:32:26,845 --> 00:32:28,643
I feel sorry for you.

476
00:32:30,449 --> 00:32:33,578
- Will you still be my attorney?
- Oh, is that why you confessed?

477
00:32:33,752 --> 00:32:35,482
Oh, right, I'm gonna
get myself disbarred,

478
00:32:35,654 --> 00:32:36,814
and I'll probably go to jail,

479
00:32:36,955 --> 00:32:39,275
- to keep you as my lawyer.
- You knew this would come out.

480
00:32:39,424 --> 00:32:41,024
You're making the
most of the inevitable.

481
00:32:41,193 --> 00:32:44,129
I am not trying to impress you.

482
00:32:45,564 --> 00:32:48,500
I'm trying to redeem me.

483
00:32:48,667 --> 00:32:52,468
I saw myself through your
eyes today, and it made me sick.

484
00:32:54,739 --> 00:32:55,763
Oh, what's the use?

485
00:32:55,941 --> 00:32:57,901
You don't believe me,
and I don't blame you a bit.

486
00:32:58,043 --> 00:32:59,944
I'll just get somebody else.

487
00:33:00,111 --> 00:33:02,512
George Knight. Remember him?

488
00:33:02,681 --> 00:33:05,776
He does a lot of criminal
work. I'll just give him a call.

489
00:33:15,861 --> 00:33:16,954
Let me think about it.

490
00:33:26,972 --> 00:33:28,497
Peter.

491
00:33:30,108 --> 00:33:32,600
Mr. Matlock called and
told me. Are you all right?

492
00:33:32,777 --> 00:33:35,713
- I've been so worried about you.
- I'm okay.

493
00:33:48,159 --> 00:33:51,596
The allegation is outrageous,
slanderous and totally unfounded,

494
00:33:51,763 --> 00:33:53,823
and beyond that, I
have no comment.

495
00:33:53,999 --> 00:33:55,023
Hi.

496
00:33:55,200 --> 00:33:58,637
Leanne Mclntyre, Peter
Mclntyre's attorney.

497
00:33:58,803 --> 00:34:00,101
Your assistant was busy.

498
00:34:00,272 --> 00:34:02,764
Well, matter of fact, everybody
out there is pretty busy.

499
00:34:02,941 --> 00:34:04,910
I guess your complicity
in the Prentice case

500
00:34:05,076 --> 00:34:06,669
has caused quite
a stir, hasn't it?

501
00:34:06,845 --> 00:34:10,145
Peter Mclntyre is a
desperate, babbling idiot.

502
00:34:10,315 --> 00:34:12,250
There's not a word of
truth in what he says.

503
00:34:12,417 --> 00:34:14,977
No? Then why did you try to
get him to leave the country?

504
00:34:15,153 --> 00:34:16,678
I don't know what
you're talking about.

505
00:34:16,855 --> 00:34:18,756
Ten years ago, you
framed Lloyd Prentice

506
00:34:18,924 --> 00:34:22,383
to try to protect the man you
hired to rob your own bank.

507
00:34:22,561 --> 00:34:24,723
Now you're trying to
make Peter look guilty

508
00:34:24,896 --> 00:34:28,492
to take the heat off the man
who actually killed Brian Morell.

509
00:34:28,667 --> 00:34:29,794
That would be you.

510
00:34:31,970 --> 00:34:34,405
You're saying I
killed him at Peter's,

511
00:34:34,573 --> 00:34:36,667
knowing it would
eventually lead back to me?

512
00:34:36,875 --> 00:34:38,343
Mm-hm.

513
00:34:38,610 --> 00:34:40,806
You may leave now, Ms. Mclntyre.

514
00:34:40,979 --> 00:34:42,845
Where you were
between 7 and 7:30

515
00:34:43,014 --> 00:34:44,744
on the evening
Mr. Morell was murdered?

516
00:34:44,916 --> 00:34:46,043
You're trespassing.

517
00:34:46,818 --> 00:34:48,787
Where were you, Mr. Ross?

518
00:34:48,954 --> 00:34:52,618
Natalie, send a security
guard to my office immediately.

519
00:34:54,059 --> 00:34:57,291
The DA is going
through Mr. Morell's files

520
00:34:57,462 --> 00:35:00,660
paragraph by paragraph
even as we speak.

521
00:35:00,832 --> 00:35:02,596
It'd be better to own
up to everything now

522
00:35:02,767 --> 00:35:05,794
before he finds
something, believe me.

523
00:35:06,605 --> 00:35:10,167
He's not going to find
anything, believe me.

524
00:35:13,945 --> 00:35:15,470
Bye.

525
00:35:44,376 --> 00:35:47,175
Can you believe
William Ross showed up?

526
00:35:48,613 --> 00:35:52,482
Look at him, consoling Mrs.
Morell like everything's hunky-dory.

527
00:35:52,651 --> 00:35:55,644
He's just trying to act off
like he has nothing to hide.

528
00:35:56,221 --> 00:35:57,587
The DA hasn't found anything

529
00:35:57,756 --> 00:36:00,419
that would justify
reopening the Prentice case.

530
00:36:00,592 --> 00:36:03,357
- He will. LEANNE:
What if he doesn't?

531
00:36:03,528 --> 00:36:07,624
Well, then Peter won't have to
stand trial for obstruction of justice.

532
00:36:07,799 --> 00:36:10,928
Yeah, he'll just stand trial
for murder and be convicted.

533
00:36:11,102 --> 00:36:12,695
Well...

534
00:36:13,672 --> 00:36:16,073
Come have a nice juicy
hot dog. Might cheer you up.

535
00:36:16,241 --> 00:36:18,210
Always cheers me up.

536
00:36:30,655 --> 00:36:33,386
Wonder what that was all about.

537
00:36:33,992 --> 00:36:36,359
Well, I guess Mrs. Morell didn't
much like that young woman

538
00:36:36,795 --> 00:36:39,731
coming to her husband's funeral.

539
00:36:41,433 --> 00:36:43,197
Pretty too.

540
00:36:44,169 --> 00:36:46,138
Came by herself.

541
00:36:46,304 --> 00:36:48,500
Think she's a relative?

542
00:36:49,207 --> 00:36:51,904
She didn't seem to know anybody.

543
00:36:52,811 --> 00:36:55,645
You think she was his mistress?

544
00:36:56,581 --> 00:36:59,449
Well, it's hard to say.

545
00:37:00,185 --> 00:37:04,646
Mrs. Morell told me her
husband never cheated on her.

546
00:37:24,109 --> 00:37:26,203
That's it, okay. Now,
back to the right.

547
00:37:26,377 --> 00:37:28,005
What? MAN 1: The right.

548
00:37:36,387 --> 00:37:40,154
- How tall was Brian Morell?
- About your height.

549
00:37:40,325 --> 00:37:41,918
Little tight.

550
00:37:44,696 --> 00:37:47,461
Well, anyway,
Mr. Morell gets in his car

551
00:37:47,632 --> 00:37:50,227
and drives over
to Peter's place.

552
00:37:50,401 --> 00:37:53,235
Parks in the
driveway, goes inside.

553
00:37:53,838 --> 00:37:56,433
Peter's at an appointment,
and his girlfriend, Cheryl, is...

554
00:37:56,608 --> 00:37:58,907
- Out to dinner with the Willeses.
- Yeah.

555
00:37:59,811 --> 00:38:03,179
The only person that's
in the house is the killer.

556
00:38:03,348 --> 00:38:05,874
He waits for Mr. Morell
to come in, turn his back,

557
00:38:06,417 --> 00:38:09,785
stabs him, then
the killer leaves.

558
00:38:09,954 --> 00:38:12,219
Then Peter drives up,
sees Mr. Morell's car,

559
00:38:12,390 --> 00:38:15,519
goes in the house and
finds the body and...

560
00:38:15,693 --> 00:38:18,959
We're not getting
anywhere with this, are we?

561
00:38:20,398 --> 00:38:22,026
Wonder if the police saw these.

562
00:38:24,235 --> 00:38:26,136
I don't know.

563
00:38:26,538 --> 00:38:27,631
Hm.

564
00:38:29,140 --> 00:38:30,335
Ugh.

565
00:38:30,875 --> 00:38:33,902
These are Brian Morell's
glasses, aren't they?

566
00:38:35,780 --> 00:38:38,011
His eyesight was
getting pretty bad.

567
00:38:38,449 --> 00:38:39,781
Mm-hm.

568
00:38:40,151 --> 00:38:41,585
But if they were his,

569
00:38:41,753 --> 00:38:44,086
what are they doing
on the passenger side?

570
00:38:54,399 --> 00:38:56,061
How you doing?

571
00:38:58,069 --> 00:39:00,061
- Well, there's Maple.
- Yeah.

572
00:39:01,039 --> 00:39:04,237
If I were driving back to
the Morells' from Peter's,

573
00:39:04,409 --> 00:39:07,072
I believe I'd go down Woodruff
Avenue to Houston Street.

574
00:39:07,245 --> 00:39:10,943
But if I were walking,

575
00:39:11,783 --> 00:39:13,342
I believe I'd go down Maple.

576
00:39:13,518 --> 00:39:16,113
- What do you think?
- Yup.

577
00:39:16,287 --> 00:39:18,256
Let's go down Maple.

578
00:39:31,502 --> 00:39:34,768
Hold it. Stop
right there, please.

579
00:39:34,939 --> 00:39:36,100
What's wrong?

580
00:39:36,274 --> 00:39:38,834
You crossed the street between
two controlled intersections.

581
00:39:39,010 --> 00:39:41,377
- Afraid that's illegal.
- Oh, we were jaywalking.

582
00:39:41,546 --> 00:39:45,608
- Yes, ma'am. Your license, please.
- Oh, for Pete's sake.

583
00:39:45,783 --> 00:39:47,513
Afraid I'll need yours too, sir.

584
00:39:48,052 --> 00:39:49,748
What do you need
our license for?

585
00:39:49,921 --> 00:39:53,050
We were just
jaywalking, not jaydriving.

586
00:39:53,224 --> 00:39:54,385
Dad.

587
00:39:54,559 --> 00:39:56,460
Need to put your
address on the ticket.

588
00:39:56,628 --> 00:39:59,325
- Ticket?
- Thirty-eight-dollar fine.

589
00:39:59,497 --> 00:40:03,935
Thirty-eight dollars for
crossing a lousy street?

590
00:40:04,102 --> 00:40:08,540
- Your license, please?
- Maybe I don't have a license.

591
00:40:08,706 --> 00:40:10,572
Maybe my wallet got stolen

592
00:40:10,742 --> 00:40:12,404
while I was being
robbed and beaten up

593
00:40:12,577 --> 00:40:15,570
by this gang of vicious
thugs who got away

594
00:40:15,747 --> 00:40:20,048
because you were in hot
pursuit of some vicious jaywalkers.

595
00:40:20,585 --> 00:40:23,646
Don't give me a hard time.
We're not doing this just for fun.

596
00:40:23,821 --> 00:40:25,813
Jaywalking on all
these streets is illegal.

597
00:40:25,990 --> 00:40:27,151
You should know that.

598
00:40:27,325 --> 00:40:29,624
Especially this close
to town, with traffic.

599
00:40:29,794 --> 00:40:31,820
That's why it's illegal.

600
00:40:32,897 --> 00:40:35,526
- Illegal?
- Yes.

601
00:40:36,901 --> 00:40:37,925
Cracking down.

602
00:40:38,970 --> 00:40:40,632
Just trying to
enforce the law, sir.

603
00:40:43,908 --> 00:40:47,276
- How much?
- Thirty-eight dollars, Mr. Matlock.

604
00:40:47,445 --> 00:40:49,607
Thirty-eight dollars.

605
00:40:49,781 --> 00:40:55,118
Always proud to give my fair
share to the town of Well Springs.

606
00:41:02,160 --> 00:41:04,000
Did you love your
husband, Mrs. Morell?

607
00:41:04,529 --> 00:41:06,259
Yes, I did.

608
00:41:06,431 --> 00:41:09,458
Did you love him in
spite of his infidelity?

609
00:41:10,835 --> 00:41:12,770
Well, my husband
wasn't unfaithful.

610
00:41:13,538 --> 00:41:15,473
Ms. Corbett, would
you please stand?

611
00:41:19,077 --> 00:41:21,205
You recognize her, don't you?

612
00:41:21,379 --> 00:41:24,178
She'd been having an affair
with your husband, hadn't she?

613
00:41:25,083 --> 00:41:26,107
Yes.

614
00:41:27,852 --> 00:41:29,286
Now...

615
00:41:34,625 --> 00:41:36,526
did you love your husband?

616
00:41:37,528 --> 00:41:41,329
- Yes, I loved him.
- You didn't resent his affair?

617
00:41:41,499 --> 00:41:43,730
Yes, but I still loved him.

618
00:41:43,901 --> 00:41:46,735
And when you heard that
his health was deteriorating,

619
00:41:46,904 --> 00:41:49,339
that he was sick and
just going to get sicker,

620
00:41:49,507 --> 00:41:52,033
that it was possible that you
would be playing nursemaid

621
00:41:52,210 --> 00:41:55,510
for several years to a
man who had betrayed you,

622
00:41:55,680 --> 00:41:56,704
did you resent that?

623
00:41:56,881 --> 00:42:01,478
- Objection. Relevance?
- Motive and opportunity, Your Honor.

624
00:42:01,652 --> 00:42:04,781
My client wasn't the
only one that had them.

625
00:42:04,956 --> 00:42:06,754
I'll allow it.

626
00:42:06,924 --> 00:42:08,044
Answer the question, please.

627
00:42:08,993 --> 00:42:12,293
No, I did not resent
it. I loved my husband.

628
00:42:13,364 --> 00:42:16,391
A pair of your husband's
glasses was found in the pocket

629
00:42:16,567 --> 00:42:20,026
on the passenger-side
door of his car.

630
00:42:20,204 --> 00:42:21,763
Not on the driver's side.

631
00:42:21,939 --> 00:42:23,237
His eyesight had gotten so bad,

632
00:42:23,408 --> 00:42:25,502
he wasn't allowed to
drive anymore, was he?

633
00:42:25,676 --> 00:42:26,735
No, he wasn't.

634
00:42:27,278 --> 00:42:28,337
How did he get around?

635
00:42:29,547 --> 00:42:30,708
I drove him.

636
00:42:31,315 --> 00:42:32,915
That's why you went
with him that morning

637
00:42:33,084 --> 00:42:35,076
with Peter to his
meeting, isn't it?

638
00:42:35,253 --> 00:42:37,017
- You drove him.
- Yes.

639
00:42:37,188 --> 00:42:40,090
Just like you drove him over to
the Mclntyre house that evening?

640
00:42:40,258 --> 00:42:42,386
No, no, I didn't.

641
00:42:43,327 --> 00:42:45,125
Well, then who did?

642
00:42:45,296 --> 00:42:47,416
His eyes were so bad, he
couldn't have driven himself,

643
00:42:47,565 --> 00:42:49,056
especially at night.

644
00:42:49,233 --> 00:42:51,600
But his car was in the driveway,
so somebody drove him.

645
00:42:51,769 --> 00:42:53,049
I didn't go anywhere that night.

646
00:42:53,204 --> 00:42:54,844
You didn't tell him
that Peter had called,

647
00:42:54,972 --> 00:42:57,212
saying he needed to see your
husband as soon as possible?

648
00:42:57,375 --> 00:42:58,695
- No, I didn't.
- You didn't drive

649
00:42:58,843 --> 00:43:00,641
to the Mclntyre house,
go into the kitchen,

650
00:43:00,812 --> 00:43:02,692
pull out a butcher knife,
and stab your husband?

651
00:43:02,847 --> 00:43:04,291
- No, I did not.
- Objection.

652
00:43:04,315 --> 00:43:06,393
You wouldn't have to take
care of a disloyal man...

653
00:43:06,417 --> 00:43:08,818
- Sustained.
- That is not true.

654
00:43:08,986 --> 00:43:11,717
Proof, Ms. Mclntyre. Now.

655
00:43:14,025 --> 00:43:15,960
Right here, Your Honor.

656
00:43:20,832 --> 00:43:24,826
- You say you were home all evening.
- I was home all night.

657
00:43:25,002 --> 00:43:28,404
My housekeeper will tell
you. I'm sure she heard the TV.

658
00:43:29,006 --> 00:43:31,066
This is a photocopy
of a traffic citation.

659
00:43:31,609 --> 00:43:32,941
It's for jaywalking,

660
00:43:33,111 --> 00:43:36,104
issued at 7:43 p.m. the evening
your husband was murdered.

661
00:43:36,280 --> 00:43:39,114
Issued to Gloria
Katherine Morell.

662
00:43:39,283 --> 00:43:42,811
Your name, your
address, your ticket.

663
00:43:43,921 --> 00:43:45,321
Right, Mrs. Morell?

664
00:43:46,958 --> 00:43:49,393
You were walking
home, weren't you?

665
00:43:49,560 --> 00:43:52,621
You had just killed your
husband, Brian, and framed Peter,

666
00:43:52,797 --> 00:43:54,823
who'd been arguing
with him that morning.

667
00:43:54,999 --> 00:43:57,366
You left Brian's
car in the driveway.

668
00:43:57,535 --> 00:43:58,735
Couldn't very well take a cab,

669
00:43:58,903 --> 00:44:01,429
because somebody
might remember you.

670
00:44:02,406 --> 00:44:04,034
And you walked home.

671
00:44:04,208 --> 00:44:05,232
But along the way,

672
00:44:05,409 --> 00:44:08,811
you got a jaywalking ticket
crossing Woodruff Avenue.

673
00:44:09,947 --> 00:44:12,610
You know what's ironic, Gloria?

674
00:44:13,451 --> 00:44:18,685
Had your husband told you the
truth, that he only had ten weeks to live,

675
00:44:18,856 --> 00:44:22,315
as opposed to two to five years,

676
00:44:23,294 --> 00:44:25,763
none of this would
have happened,

677
00:44:27,798 --> 00:44:29,391
would it?

678
00:44:33,304 --> 00:44:35,398
Nothing further, Your Honor.

679
00:44:41,212 --> 00:44:46,116
The jury finds the defendant,
Peter Mclntyre, not guilty.

680
00:44:47,118 --> 00:44:49,713
Thank you, ladies and
gentlemen of the jury.

681
00:44:49,887 --> 00:44:51,685
Court is adjourned.

682
00:44:52,590 --> 00:44:55,822
Thanks, Leanne. You're ten
times the lawyer I'll ever be.

683
00:44:56,360 --> 00:44:57,953
I know.

684
00:45:04,335 --> 00:45:05,997
Oh, and thank you, Ben.

685
00:45:06,170 --> 00:45:09,265
Don't thank me. It was all
Leanne. I would never have done it.

686
00:45:09,440 --> 00:45:11,400
Be that as it may, it all
worked out for the best.

687
00:45:11,542 --> 00:45:13,272
- So come on, now, be a sport, huh?
- Sure.

688
00:45:19,050 --> 00:45:20,484
Let's go.

689
00:45:25,423 --> 00:45:26,447
Bad news.

690
00:45:26,624 --> 00:45:29,264
Jim just said the DA's on the
verge of reopening the Prentice case.

691
00:45:29,427 --> 00:45:32,158
- What did they find?
- Brian Morell's safe-deposit box,

692
00:45:32,330 --> 00:45:34,629
with records showing
three people were paid to lie

693
00:45:34,799 --> 00:45:38,099
about Lloyd Prentice's
whereabouts the time of the robbery.

694
00:45:38,269 --> 00:45:42,866
- William Ross will get his.
- And Peter. Poor Peter.

695
00:45:44,408 --> 00:45:46,707
That mean you're
taking the case?

696
00:45:46,877 --> 00:45:48,778
He confessed.

697
00:45:48,946 --> 00:45:50,539
Is that a no?

698
00:45:50,715 --> 00:45:52,775
- Yes.
- Oh, good.

699
00:45:52,950 --> 00:45:54,851
Let's go home.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
